    "description": "A Hellenistic theater located in the ancient Greek colony of Tauric Chersonesos. Built in the 4th or 3rd century BC, it remained in use through the Roman period. It is one of the few surviving examples of Greek theater architecture in the northern Black Sea region.",
    "details": "<p>The Greek theater at Chersonesos dates to approximately the 4th or 3rd century BC and represents the cultural vitality of this northern outpost of the Hellenistic world. The theater was constructed in the standard Hellenistic style, featuring a stone cavea (seating area), an orchestra, and a stone stage building. It was built largely of local limestone blocks and is estimated to have accommodated between 1,800 and 2,000 spectators. Its design reflects close ties to the architectural traditions of mainland Greece and the broader Hellenistic world, adapted to the local topography of the coastal site. As in other Greek cities, the theater served multiple civic and religious functions, including dramatic performances, musical and poetry competitions, and public assemblies. The theater remained in active use into the 4th century AD. With the adoption of Christianity in late antiquity, the theater fell out of use and was gradually dismantled. By the 6th century AD, a Christian basilica had been constructed over part of the site, a pattern common across the former Roman and Byzantine world. Archaeological excavations in the 19th and 20th centuries have revealed substantial remains of the theater's foundations, seating, and stage area. The structure is noted among the features listed for Chersonesos on the UNESCO World Heritage list (the site was inscribed in 2013 as the \"Ancient City of Tauric Chersonese and its Chora\").</p>",
